enum UXDesignerStudyBank {
    static let sections: [StudySection] = [
        // Section 1: Provided with a set of business requirements, determine whic (8 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem",
            title: "Provided with a set of business requirements, determine which research methodology and tools should be used to design a solution.",
            color: Color(red: 0.30, green: 0.50, blue: 0.80),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.94, blue: 1.00),
            icon: "doc.text.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_obj",
                    title: "Provided with a set of business requirements, determine which research methodology and tools should be used to design a solution.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_1", number: 1, question: "Selecting the correct research approach (generative vs. evaluative) depending on whether the team is exploring a problem space or validating a proposed solution.", answer: "Generative research is used when the team does not yet know what problem to solve. Methods include interviews, ethnographic observation, and diary studies. The output is insights and problem definitions. Evaluative research is used when a solution already exists (even as a prototype) and the team needs to assess how well it works. Methods include usability testing and A/B testing. Key rule: if the team is asking 'What should we build?' use generative. If asking 'Does this work?' use evaluative."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_2", number: 2, question: "Choosing between qualitative research methods (contextual inquiry, interviews, diary studies) vs. quantitative methods (surveys, analytics) given a specific business constraint such as limited access to users or need for statistical confidence.", answer: "Qualitative methods (interviews, contextual inquiry, diary studies) reveal the 'why' behind behaviors. They require fewer participants (5-10) but need direct user access. Use them to uncover motivations, mental models, and pain points. Quantitative methods (surveys, analytics) reveal the 'what' and 'how many.' They require larger samples for statistical significance and can be run without live user access. If you have limited user access, analytics and surveys are practical alternatives. If you need to understand root causes, qualitative methods are essential even with access constraints."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_3", number: 3, question: "Defining research objectives and writing a discussion guide for a stakeholder or user interview, including how to structure open-ended questions to avoid leading the participant.", answer: "A research objective defines what you need to learn (e.g., 'Understand how sales reps currently track follow-up activities'). A discussion guide is a structured but flexible script with warm-up questions, core topic questions, and wrap-up. Open-ended questions begin with 'Tell me about...', 'Walk me through...', or 'How do you...'. Avoid leading questions like 'Don't you find it hard to...?' or binary yes/no questions. Probing follow-ups such as 'Can you say more about that?' surface deeper insights without steering the participant."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_4", number: 4, question: "Selecting the appropriate discovery tool (empathy maps, journey maps, stakeholder maps, affinity diagrams) based on the research goal and available inputs.", answer: "Empathy maps (Think/Feel/Say/Do) synthesize interview data into a shared understanding of a user's mindset. Journey maps visualize the end-to-end experience across touchpoints and reveal moments of friction. Stakeholder maps identify who influences the project and their level of interest/power. Affinity diagrams cluster raw research observations into themes to surface patterns. Choose based on what you have: raw interview notes -> affinity diagram; need to understand a user's emotional experience -> empathy map; need to see the full flow of a process -> journey map."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_5", number: 5, question: "Determining appropriate participant screening criteria and recruitment strategies when end users are difficult to access directly (e.g., using proxy users, SMEs, or customer success managers as substitutes).", answer: "Screening criteria define who qualifies as a valid research participant (e.g., role, tenure, usage frequency). When direct end-user access is blocked, proxy users, subject matter experts (SMEs), or customer success managers who interact with users daily can serve as substitutes. Proxies provide second-hand insights, so triangulate their input with any available analytics data. Always document proxy limitations in your research findings so stakeholders understand the gap."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_6", number: 6, question: "Using competitive analysis or benchmarking against industry-standard UX patterns to establish baseline design requirements when no direct user access is available.", answer: "Competitive analysis reviews how similar products solve the same problem, revealing industry norms users may already expect. Benchmarking compares a proposed design against established UX heuristics or recognized pattern libraries (e.g., Nielsen Norman Group, SLDS component blueprints). Both are useful when you cannot recruit users: they give you a defensible baseline for design decisions grounded in existing user expectations rather than assumptions."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_7", number: 7, question: "Determining when to use card sorting vs. tree testing for evaluating information architecture decisions.", answer: "Card sorting asks participants to group content cards into categories that make sense to them. It is used early, when you are designing or redesigning a navigation structure, to understand users' mental models. Tree testing (also called 'reverse card sorting') asks participants to find items within an existing hierarchy. It validates whether a proposed navigation structure is intuitive. Use card sorting to create IA; use tree testing to validate it."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s1_provided_with_a_set_of_business_requirem_8", number: 8, question: "Identifying the appropriate use of usability testing vs. A/B testing given constraints around sample size, development phase, and decision type.", answer: "Usability testing observes users completing tasks and identifies why problems occur. It requires fewer participants (5-8), works at any fidelity level, and surfaces qualitative insight. A/B testing compares two live variants with a large audience to determine which performs better on a measurable metric. Use usability testing in discovery or design phases to find and fix problems. Use A/B testing in production when you need statistically significant data on which of two working solutions drives better outcomes.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 2: Given a scenario, demonstrate how to gather requirements and (7 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath",
            title: "Given a scenario, demonstrate how to gather requirements and plan strategy given current user experiences.",
            color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.60, blue: 0.50),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.96, blue: 0.94),
            icon: "checkmark.shield.fill",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, demonstrate how to gather requirements and plan strategy given current user experiences.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_1", number: 1, question: "Using stakeholder interviews and contextual observation to surface hidden pain points that stakeholders cannot articulate in written requirements.", answer: "Stakeholders often describe solutions ('we need a button here') rather than problems. Interviews using open-ended questions reveal the underlying need. Contextual observation (watching users work in their actual environment) uncovers workarounds, shortcuts, and frustrations that users have normalized and would never mention in a meeting. Together, these methods surface the real requirements behind stated ones."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_2", number: 2, question: "Identifying when existing analytics data (page views, drop-off rates, click paths) is sufficient to define design requirements vs. when primary research is still required.", answer: "Analytics data is sufficient when it clearly answers 'what is happening' and the decision is straightforward (e.g., a page with a 90% drop-off rate clearly needs redesign). Primary research is still required when analytics shows a problem but not the cause (e.g., users abandon a form, but you don't know why). The rule: analytics defines the 'what'; interviews and observation reveal the 'why.' Use both together for the strongest requirements base."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_3", number: 3, question: "Synthesizing research findings using affinity mapping to cluster themes and identify the most impactful problem areas to address in a Salesforce solution.", answer: "Affinity mapping involves writing individual observations, quotes, or data points on sticky notes (physical or digital) and grouping them by similarity. Clusters that emerge represent recurring themes. In a Salesforce context, common themes might be around data entry friction, navigation confusion, or lack of visibility into related records. Once clustered, themes are ranked by frequency and severity to prioritize the Salesforce configuration or design changes that will have the most impact."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_4", number: 4, question: "Building an as-is journey map from interview and observation data to identify moments of friction in the current user experience.", answer: "An as-is journey map documents what users actually do today, step by step, including the systems they use, their actions, their emotional highs and lows, and their pain points at each stage. It is built from interview quotes and observation notes. In Salesforce projects, this reveals where users switch between systems, perform manual workarounds, or duplicate data entry, which then become the targets for declarative or process improvements."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_5", number: 5, question: "Translating raw user research findings into a prioritized problem statement (How Might We statement) to guide design strategy.", answer: "A How Might We (HMW) statement reframes a user pain point as a design opportunity. Format: 'How might we [help user] [achieve goal] [given constraint]?' Example: 'How might we help service agents find related case history without leaving the active case record?' HMWs are derived from affinity-mapped research themes and serve as the design brief. They are broad enough to allow creative solutions but specific enough to stay grounded in the real user problem."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_6", number: 6, question: "Aligning stakeholders around research findings by presenting evidence (quotes, observations, data) in a compelling format that builds organizational support for a UX-centered approach.", answer: "Stakeholders respond to evidence, not opinions. Present findings using a mix of direct user quotes, video clips (if available), behavioral data, and journey maps. Frame issues in terms of business impact (e.g., 'Users spend 12 minutes per case switching systems, translating to X hours per week across the team'). Use a structured readout with themes, supporting evidence, and prioritized recommendations. This transforms research into a shared organizational understanding that justifies UX investment."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s2_given_a_scenario_demonstrate_how_to_gath_7", number: 7, question: "Conducting a heuristic evaluation against Nielsen's 10 heuristics to identify UX deficiencies in an existing Salesforce implementation.", answer: "Nielsen's 10 heuristics are expert-review criteria: (1) Visibility of system status, (2) Match between system and real world, (3) User control and freedom, (4) Consistency and standards, (5) Error prevention, (6) Recognition over recall, (7) Flexibility and efficiency, (8) Aesthetic and minimalist design, (9) Help users recognize and recover from errors, (10) Help and documentation. Evaluators walk through a Salesforce implementation and flag violations per heuristic with a severity rating. No user recruitment is needed, making it fast and cost-effective for identifying obvious problems.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 3: Describe key Salesforce UX personas and which processes they (4 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s3_describe_key_salesforce_ux_personas_and_",
            title: "Describe key Salesforce UX personas and which processes they normally do.",
            color: Color(red: 0.55, green: 0.35, blue: 0.75),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.94, green: 0.90, blue: 0.98),
