            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s3_describe_key_salesforce_ux_personas_and__obj",
                    title: "Describe key Salesforce UX personas and which processes they normally do.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_describe_key_salesforce_ux_personas_and__1", number: 1, question: "Differentiating the responsibilities and platform behaviors of the Salesforce Admin, End User, and Executive personas in the context of UX design.", answer: "The Admin configures and maintains the org: manages users, profiles, permission sets, page layouts, flows, and custom objects. Their UX pain points are around setup complexity and change management. The End User performs daily business tasks (logging activities, managing records, running reports). Their UX needs center on efficiency, clarity, and minimal friction. The Executive reviews dashboards and reports for KPIs and strategic decision-making. Their UX needs are around data visibility and fast, high-level insights. Design decisions must account for all three."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_describe_key_salesforce_ux_personas_and__2", number: 2, question: "Mapping Salesforce standard personas (Trailblazer, Admin, End User) to their typical Salesforce platform touchpoints and pain points.", answer: "The Trailblazer persona is a champion of Salesforce adoption, often an advanced user or internal advocate who explores new features and shares knowledge. Touchpoints: Trailhead, release notes, community groups. The Admin's touchpoints include Setup, App Manager, Flow Builder, and Object Manager. Pain points: too many customization requests, governance challenges. The End User's touchpoints are Lightning record pages, list views, reports, and mobile app. Pain points: too many fields, confusing navigation, slow page loads."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_describe_key_salesforce_ux_personas_and__3", number: 3, question: "Identifying which Salesforce UX persona is best represented by a given job description or workflow scenario (e.g., configuring flows vs. logging activities vs. reviewing dashboards).", answer: "Configuring flows, page layouts, or permission sets: Admin persona. Logging calls, updating opportunity stages, or creating cases: End User persona. Reviewing pipeline dashboards, win-rate reports, or executive summaries: Executive persona. On the exam, look for action verbs: 'configure/set up/manage' = Admin; 'log/update/submit/close' = End User; 'review/monitor/approve' = Executive."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s3_describe_key_salesforce_ux_personas_and__4", number: 4, question: "Constructing a UX persona document from research data, including goals, frustrations, behaviors, and technology comfort level, to guide Salesforce configuration decisions.", answer: "A UX persona is a research-grounded archetype representing a key user group. It includes: name and role, primary goals (what they are trying to accomplish), frustrations (recurring pain points), typical workflows and touchpoints, technology comfort level, and a representative quote. In Salesforce, personas drive configuration decisions: a low-tech-comfort persona signals the need for simpler page layouts and more in-app guidance, while a power user persona might justify a console app and advanced quick actions.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 4: Given a scenario, describe how Salesforce can add value to u (3 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s4_given_a_scenario_describe_how_salesforce",
            title: "Given a scenario, describe how Salesforce can add value to user experiences.",
            color: Color(red: 0.80, green: 0.40, blue: 0.20),
            lightColor: Color(red: 1.00, green: 0.94, blue: 0.88),
            icon: "chart.bar.xaxis.ascending",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s4_given_a_scenario_describe_how_salesforce_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, describe how Salesforce can add value to user experiences.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_describe_how_salesforce_1", number: 1, question: "Recognizing how Salesforce's contextual data (related lists, activity timelines, embedded analytics) reduces user effort compared to switching between external systems.", answer: "Salesforce consolidates related data on a single record page. Related lists surface child records (e.g., Contacts on an Account) without navigation. The Activity Timeline shows emails, calls, and tasks in chronological order. Embedded dashboards or report charts surface analytics in context. All of these reduce 'system switching,' where users must open multiple tools to get a complete picture. Fewer context switches means less cognitive load and faster task completion."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_describe_how_salesforce_2", number: 2, question: "Identifying which Salesforce platform capability (automation, collaboration, analytics, guided selling) provides the most value given a described business problem.", answer: "Automation (Flow, Process Builder triggers): best when users repeat the same manual steps or data entry. Collaboration (Chatter, Slack integration): best when users need to coordinate across teams on a record. Analytics (Reports, Dashboards, Einstein Analytics): best when users need visibility into trends or performance. Guided selling (Path, Prompts, Screen Flows): best when users need coaching through a multi-step process or decision. Match the capability to the underlying problem, not just the stated request."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s4_given_a_scenario_describe_how_salesforce_3", number: 3, question: "Evaluating whether a requested customization adds genuine user value or introduces unnecessary complexity given a described current-state experience.", answer: "Not every customization request improves UX. Ask: Does this reduce steps, eliminate confusion, or surface needed information? Or does it add fields, buttons, or logic that most users will never use? A useful test is to evaluate the customization against the user's primary task. If it directly supports completing that task faster or more accurately, it adds value. If it satisfies an edge case at the cost of cluttering the interface for the majority, it introduces unnecessary complexity.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

        // Section 5: Given a scenario, identify which UX method should be used to (9 topics)
        StudySection(
            id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho",
            title: "Given a scenario, identify which UX method should be used to define a user experience.",
            color: Color(red: 0.25, green: 0.55, blue: 0.70),
            lightColor: Color(red: 0.88, green: 0.95, blue: 0.98),
            icon: "globe",
            objectives: [
                StudyObjective(
                    id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_obj",
                    title: "Given a scenario, identify which UX method should be used to define a user experience.",
                    topics: [
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_1", number: 1, question: "Applying design thinking phases (Empathize, Define, Ideate, Prototype, Test) to determine the correct next action given a described project scenario.", answer: "Empathize: research users through interviews, observation, and empathy maps. Define: synthesize findings into a clear problem statement (HMW statement, POV statement). Ideate: brainstorm solutions without judgment; use techniques like crazy 8s or how-might-we prompts. Prototype: build a low-fidelity representation (wireframe, clickable mockup) to make ideas tangible. Test: put the prototype in front of users and observe. On the exam, identify which phase applies based on what the team knows and what they need to learn next."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_2", number: 2, question: "Selecting the correct UX deliverable (persona, journey map, wireframe, prototype, user story) based on the project phase and the audience receiving it.", answer: "Persona: communicates who the user is; audience is the team and stakeholders. Journey map: communicates the current or desired end-to-end experience; audience is stakeholders and product owners. Wireframe: communicates structure and layout; audience is developers and stakeholders. Prototype: communicates interaction and flow; audience is users (for testing) and developers. User story: communicates a feature requirement from the user's perspective; audience is development team. Match deliverable to audience and phase: early phases favor personas and journey maps; design phases favor wireframes and prototypes; delivery phases use user stories."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_3", number: 3, question: "The four levels of UX fidelity: wireframe (structure only), mockup (visual design added), prototype (interactive), and live build — and the defining characteristic of each.", answer: "Wireframe: grayscale boxes and lines; shows layout and structure, no visual design. Fast and cheap to create. Mockup: adds real colors, typography, and imagery; looks like the final product but is static. Prototype: interactive; users can click through screens and experience the flow. Live build: actual working software in a staging or production environment. The key differentiator is interactivity: wireframes and mockups are static; prototypes are interactive; live builds are functional."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_4", number: 4, question: "Matching UX fidelity level (wireframe, mockup, prototype, live build) to the correct validation goal given a described scenario.", answer: "Wireframe: validates information architecture and layout decisions. Mockup: validates visual design, brand alignment, and aesthetic. Prototype: validates navigation flow, task completion, and interaction patterns. Live build: validates end-to-end performance, accessibility, and real-world usability. If a scenario asks 'what should you build to test whether users can find the correct button,' a prototype is appropriate. If it asks 'what should you build to check if brand colors are applied correctly,' a mockup is sufficient."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_5", number: 5, question: "Distinguishing when to use low-fidelity wireframes vs. high-fidelity prototypes given constraints around stakeholder expectations and development timeline.", answer: "Low-fidelity wireframes are fast, cheap, and encourage feedback on structure rather than aesthetics. Use them early, when ideas are still evolving, or when time is short. High-fidelity prototypes are time-intensive but better represent the final product. Use them when stakeholders need to see a realistic preview, when testing nuanced interaction decisions, or when the design is stable and ready for validation. Beware: high-fidelity prototypes can anchor stakeholders to visual details too early, stifling design iteration."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_6", number: 6, question: "Choosing between a clickable prototype and a paper prototype for early-stage usability testing given resource constraints.", answer: "A paper prototype (hand-drawn screens) is the fastest and cheapest option. It is appropriate when time or budget is extremely limited, or when the design is so early that digital fidelity would be misleading. Changes are instant: just redraw the page. A clickable prototype (digital, interactive) is more realistic and better mimics the final experience, reducing the learning curve for participants unfamiliar with paper testing. Choose paper when speed trumps realism; choose clickable when realism is needed for valid test results."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_7", number: 7, question: "Using an empathy map (Think, Feel, Say, Do quadrants) to consolidate user interview data and reveal gaps between what users say and what they actually do.", answer: "An empathy map has four quadrants: Think (what is on the user's mind?), Feel (what emotions are they experiencing?), Say (direct quotes from interviews), Do (observed behaviors and actions). The gap between Say and Do is particularly valuable: users often report behaviors that differ from what they actually do (e.g., 'I always update the record right away' vs. observed behavior of batch-updating at end of day). This gap reveals unspoken needs and design opportunities."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_8", number: 8, question: "Selecting a future-state journey map vs. an as-is journey map based on whether the team is documenting a current pain point or designing a desired experience.", answer: "An as-is journey map documents the current reality: what users actually do today, including pain points, workarounds, and emotional lows. It is built from research data. A future-state journey map designs the ideal experience: what the user journey should look like after the solution is implemented. It is aspirational and used to align the team on a shared vision. Use as-is for problem identification; use future-state for solution alignment and design direction."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "s5_given_a_scenario_identify_which_ux_metho_9", number: 9, question: "Determining when a service blueprint adds value over a journey map when multiple internal systems and back-office processes are involved.", answer: "A journey map focuses on the user's front-stage experience. A service blueprint extends this by adding: the line of visibility (what the user sees vs. doesn't see), backstage actions (what staff do behind the scenes), support processes (internal systems, databases, third-party services). Service blueprints are essential when the user experience depends on complex back-office coordination, such as a case resolution process that involves multiple Salesforce objects, integrations, and teams. If the exam scenario involves systems or processes the user doesn't directly interact with, a service blueprint adds value.")
                    ]
                )
            ]
        ),

